#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157189 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157369 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "697e5bea-20b4-5de0-9992-7a67ee932809" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Continuous Random Variable Given a Two-Branch PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "0a870e80-5a73-59d2-997f-bec2d9a947e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7988" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac {10}{3}$, compute $\\textrm{Var}[X]$.\n${8 - 2x,\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\le x \\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\textrm E[X^2]$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{3}^{4}x^{2} \\cdot (8 - 2x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{3}^{4}(8x^{2} - 2x^{3})dx \\\\\n&= \\left.(\\frac{8x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{x^{4}}{2})\\right|_{3}^{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{67}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{67}{6} - (\\frac{10}{3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157190 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c834972a-1ed0-585c-803c-906fcea8cea8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Continuous Random Variable Given a Three-Branch PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "77bbf810-ec26-5224-9d3e-62de3e0529fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-7990" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function given below. Given that $\\textrm E[X]\\approx 1.791\\,67$, compute $\\textrm{Var}[X]$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n${\\frac{1}{4},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 2, \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{3}{4},\\end{vmatrix}2 \\le x < 3,; 0, \\mid otherwise$." :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\textrm E[X^2]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{0}^{3}x^{2} \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}x^{2} \\cdot f(x)dx + \\int_{2}^{3}x^{2} \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}dx + \\int_{2}^{3}x^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{3}{4})dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{x^{2}}{4}dx + \\int_{2}^{3}(\\frac{x^{3}}{2} - \\frac{3x^{2}}{4})dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{x^{3}}{12}\\right|_{0}^{2} + \\left.(\\frac{x^{4}}{8} - \\frac{x^{3}}{4})\\right|_{2}^{3} \\\\\n&\\approx 4.04167\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&\\approx 4.04167 - (1.79167)^{2} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.83\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}]}
